      "notes": "All claims are well-grounded. The two stated-tier claims quote the policy text verbatim. The three measurable-tier claims (E26, E35, E36) are drawn from IPPR/House of Commons Library/Parliament sources and the quotes entail the claims without overstating them \u2014 notably E26 comes from an advocacy-adjacent media source (airqualitynews.com reporting an IPPR report), which is a mild concern but the claim is descriptive and corroborated by E37. Tier integrity is sound: no projections are dressed as measurables. The direction 'improves' with 'minor' magnitude and 'low' confidence is justified by the surviving claims \u2014 the road-maintenance mechanism is concrete and the active travel savings are correctly flagged as contingent. No false balance issue: the verdict does not claim 'mixed'; it claims 'improves' with low confidence, which is appropriate given one concrete mechanism (pothole repair) and one aspirational one. No lever leakage, no party identity smuggled in. The rationale correctly distinguishes immediate relief (road repair cost savings) from long-run uncertain effects (active travel household savings). One minor note: E33 (pothole damage costs \u00a3320\u2013\u00a3500/year) is cited in the rationale but not listed as a formal claim with evidence_id \u2014 this is a pipeline completeness gap but not a blocking issue for this verdict's logic. Overall the verdict is coherent, evidence-justified, and publication-ready."
